The Human-English states Proposition 2.2: (i) \(\bot \ll x\), (ii) \(x \ll z\) and \(y \ll z\) imply \(x \sqcup y \ll z\), (iii) \(x \ll y \sqsubseteq z\) implies \(x \ll z\), (iv) \(x \sqsubseteq y \ll z\) implies \(x \ll z\), (v) \(x \ll y\) implies \(x \sqsubseteq y\), (vi) \(x \ll x\) if and only if the principal upset of \(x\) is open, and (vii) for directed \(S\), \(x \ll \bigsqcup S\) if and only if \(x \ll y\) for some \(y \in S\). The relation is defined in [the way-below relation](1972_WayBelow.md). There is no `proposition_2_2` module. `bot_wayBelow` proves (i) because the universe is a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(x\) and is contained in the upset of bottom. `WayBelow.sup` proves (ii) by intersecting the two witnessing neighbourhoods, so a point of the intersection lies above both \(x\) and \(y\) and hence above their join. `WayBelow.trans_le` proves (iii) because a Scott-open set is an upper set, so the neighbourhood of \(y\) already contains \(z\). `WayBelow.le_trans` proves (iv) because a neighbourhood contained in the upset of \(y\) is contained in the upset of \(x\). `WayBelow.le` proves (v) because \(y\) itself lies in the witnessing neighbourhood. `WayBelow.self_iff_scottOpen_Ici` proves (vi): if \(x \ll x\), a witnessing neighbourhood contained in the principal upset equals that upset, since the neighbourhood is an upper set containing \(x\), and conversely a Scott-open principal upset is a neighbourhood of \(x\) contained in itself. `WayBelow.sSup_iff` proves (vii) for a nonempty directed set, matching the paper’s convention that directed sets are nonempty: a witnessing neighbourhood of the supremum meets the set, and the same neighbourhood witnesses \(x \ll s\) at the meeting point, while the converse is monotonicity on the right because the member lies below the supremum. The later biconditional, with \(\sqsubseteq\) rather than \(\ll\) on the right and with an arbitrary directed set bounded above by \(y\), is not the statement of `WayBelow.sSup_iff`. **Theorem:** partial — clauses (i)–(vii) are formalized, and the directed-supremum characterization of \(\ll\) is not. **Proof:** yes — each clause is derived from an interior witness by the intersection, upper-set transport, or directed-meeting argument that clause requires.
